<DocsLayout title="Discover and Flatpak">
    <h1>Discover and Flatpak</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Octlitch uses Flatpak and Flathub for many graphical desktop
        applications.
    </p>

    <h2>KDE Discover</h2>

    <p>
        Discover provides a graphical way to browse, install, remove and
        update Flatpak applications.
    </p>

    <p>
        Open <strong>Discover</strong> from the application launcher and use
        the search field to find software.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>System packages are managed separately</strong>

        <p>
            Discover in Octlitch is intended for Flatpak applications.
            Use DNF in the terminal when installing or updating system
            packages.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Flathub</h2>

    <p>
        Flathub is the primary Flatpak application source used by Octlitch.
    </p>

    <p>
        Check the configured Flatpak remotes with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remotes</code></pre>

    <p>
        A normal Octlitch installation should include a Flathub remote.
    </p>

    <h2>Add Flathub manually</h2>

    <p>
        If Flathub is not configured, it can be added with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists \
flathub \
https://flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo</code></pre>

    <h2>Search for applications</h2>

    <pre><code>flatpak search application-name</code></pre>

    <p>
        Example:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak search Krita</code></pre>

    <h2>Install an application</h2>

    <p>
        Applications can be installed through Discover or directly from
        the terminal.
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak install flathub application-id</code></pre>

    <p>
        Flatpak applications use identifiers such as:
    </p>

    <pre><code>org.kde.krita</code></pre>

    <h2>List installed Flatpaks</h2>

    <pre><code>flatpak list</code></pre>

    <h2>Update Flatpak applications</h2>

    <p>
        Discover can check for application updates automatically.
        You can also update everything from the terminal:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak update</code></pre>

    <h2>Remove an application</h2>

    <pre><code>flatpak uninstall application-id</code></pre>

    <h2>Remove unused runtimes</h2>

    <p>
        Flatpak applications can share large runtimes. When applications
        are removed, unused runtimes may remain installed.
    </p>

    <p>
        Clean them with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak uninstall --unused</code></pre>

    <h2>Fedora Flatpaks</h2>

    <p>
        Octlitch does not use the Fedora OCI Flatpak remote by default.
        Flathub is used instead for the graphical application catalogue.
    </p>

    <p>
        This keeps the application source simple and avoids presenting
        duplicate versions of the same software.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-next">
        <a href="/documentation/hardware/nvidia/">
            Next: NVIDIA graphics →
        </a>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
